888	\begin{abstract}
889	Hadronic decays of boosted resonances, e.g., top quark jets, at hadronic super colliders
890	are frequent predictions in TeV-scale extensions of the Standard Model of Particle Physics.
891	In such scenarios, accurate modeling of QCD radiation is necessary for trustworthy predictions.
892	We present the automation of fully differential, next-to-leading-order (NLO) in QCD corrections with parton shower (PS) matching for 
893	an effective  Left-Right Symmetric Model (LRSM) that features $W_R^\pm, Z_R$ gauge bosons and heavy Majorana neutrinos $N$.
894	Publicly available universal model files require remarkably fewer user inputs for predicting benchmark collider processes
895	than leading order LRSM constructions. 
896	We present predictions for inclusive $W_R^\pm, Z_R$ production at the $\sqrt{s} = 13$ TeV Large Hadron Collider (LHC)
897	and a hypothetical future 100 TeV Very Large Hadron Collider (VLHC),
898	as well as inclusive $N$ production for a hypothetical Large Hadron Electron Collider (LHeC).
899	As a case study, we investigate at NLO+PS accuracy the properties of 
900	heavy neutrino (color-singlet) jets and top quark (color-triplet) jets from decays of high-mass $W_R$ bosons at the LHC.
901	Contrary to top jets, we find that the kinematic properties of heavy neutrinos jets, and in particular jet mass, 
902	are resilient against the effects of parton showers and hard QCD radiation.
903	This suggests that in searches for neutrino jets, 
904	aggressive selection cuts that would otherwise be inappropriate for top jets can be imposed with minimal signal loss.
905	\end{abstract}
906	
907	\keywords{Automation, NLO Computations, Neutrinos Mass Models, Boosted Topologies}
