1223	\section{Computational Setup and Signal Modeling}\label{sec:setup}
1224	\subsection{Model Implementation}
1225	We implement the SM Lagrangian with Goldstone boson couplings in the Feynman gauge 
1226	and the Lagrangian terms of Eqs.~(\ref{chcurrent})-(\ref{neucurrent}) in the Unitary gauge
1227	into FR 2.3.10~\cite{Alloul:2013bka,Christensen:2008py}.
1228	$R_2$ rational and QCD renormalization counter terms are calculated with~\nloct~1.02~\cite{Degrande:2014vpa} and FeynArts 3.8~\cite{Hahn:2000kx}.
1229	UFO model files are publicly available from the FR model database~\cite{nloFRModel}, 
1230	and can be ported into modern event generators, including
1231	\mgamc~\cite{Alwall:2014hca},
1232	HERWIG~\cite{Bellm:2015jjp}, and SHERPA~\cite{Gleisberg:2008ta}.
1233	
1234	\subsection{Monte Carlo Configuration}
1235	Fully differential results at LO and NLO are obtained using \mgamc~\confirm{2.5.$\beta$2}~\cite{Alwall:2014hca}.
1236	Events are parton showered and hadronized using Pythia~\confirm{8.219} (PY8)~\cite{Sjostrand:2014zea},
1237	and passed to MadAnalysis5~\confirm{v1.4}~\cite{Conte:2012fm} for particle-level clustering.
1238	Unless stated otherwise, jets are clustered via FastJet~\confirm{3.2.1}~\cite{Cacciari:2005hq,Cacciari:2011ma} 
1239	according to the anti-$k_T$ algorithm~\cite{Cacciari:2008gp} with a separation scale of $R = 0.4$.
1240	
1241	LRSM inputs are given in Eq.~(\ref{eq:lrsmInputs}). SM inputs are taken from the 2014 Particle Data Group~\cite{Agashe:2014kda}:
1242	\begin{eqnarray}
1243	 \alpha^{\rm \overline{MS}}(M_{Z}) = 1/127.940, \quad 
1244	 M_{Z} = 91.1876\GeV, \quad
1245	 \sin^{2}_{\rm \overline{MS}}(\theta_{W}) = 0.23126.
1246	 \label{eq:smInputs}
1247	\end{eqnarray}
1248	We use the NLO NNPDF3.0 parton distribution function (PDF) set~(\texttt{lhaid=260000})~\cite{Ball:2014uwa} for LO and NLO calculations.
1249	PDFs and $\as(\mu_r)$ are extracted using LHAPDF 6.1.6~\cite{Buckley:2014ana}.
1250	
1251	For all processes, we equate the renormalization $(\mu_r)$ and factorization $(\mu_f)$ scales.
1252	We choose as a dynamical scale, half the sum over all final-state transverse energies:
1253	\begin{equation}
1254	 \mu_r, \mu_f = \mu_0 \equiv \sum_{k=N,\ell,\text{jets}}\cfrac{E_{T,k}}{2} = \frac12\sum_{k} \sqrt{m_k^2 + p_{T,k}^2}
1255	 \label{eq:mgScale}
1256	\end{equation}
1257	At NLO, we estimate the residual uncertainty from missing higher order terms by simultaneously varying $\mu_{r},\mu_f$ over the range:
1258	\begin{equation}
1259	 0.5\times\mu_0 ~<~ \mu_{r},\mu_f ~<~ 2\times\mu_0.
1260	\end{equation}
1261	
1262	
1263	Instructions for using the Effective LRSM at NLO within the \mgamc~framework are provided in App.~\ref{app:sigDef}.
1264	
1265	For total inclusive cross sections reported in Sec.~\ref{sec:ppXSec} and \ref{sec:epXSec}, no phase space cuts are applied.
1266	
1267	To study the properties of $N$ jets and $t$ jets, we consider the processes 
1268	\begin{equation}
1269	 p ~p ~\rightarrow \WR^{\pm (*)} ~\rightarrow Ne^\pm, ~tb, ~\text{and}~q\overline{q'}, \quad\text{for}\quad q\in\{u,d,c,s\}, 
1270	 \label{eq:lhcNLOPSprocesses}
1271	\end{equation}
1272	at NLO in QCD. We include hard QCD radiation off the final-state quarks and a finite $\WR$ width.
1273	While we neglect interference with the SM $W$ for simplicity, it is possible to implement it within our framework.
1274	To minimize the contamination of far off-shell $\WR$ with virtualities $Q^2\ll \MWR^{2}$,
1275	we impose a generator-level cut on $X\in\{N,t,q\}$ and require $p_T^X > 750\GeV$.
1276	While the cut on light quarks is trivial in \mgamc
1277	restrictions on on-shell heavy neutrinos and top quarks require implementing a user-defined cut into the phase space integration routine.
1278	This can be done in a straightforward manner; see App.~\ref{app:GenCuts} for instructions.
1279	
1280	\subsection{Spin-Correlated Decays of $N$ and $t$ with Improved MadSpin}
1281	We decay $N$ and $t$ via MadSpin~\cite{Artoisenet:2012st}, thus retaining full spin correlation, just before parton showering.
1282	However, three body decays like $N\rightarrow \ell^\pm\WR^{\mp*}\rightarrow\ell^\pm q\overline{q'}$ are not supported in current releases of MadSpin.
1283	Therefore, we have implemented an extension of the code to support such subprocesses.
1284	
1285	To achieve this, we first use a standard MC technique to generate unweighted decay events with the parent particle being exactly on-shell.
1286	Those events are then boosted to match the decaying particle of the production event.
1287	Obviously such samples lack spin-correlations between the production event and the decay event.
1288	To include spin effects, one can re-weight each decayed event by the following ratio:
1289	\begin{equation}
1290	\cfrac{|M_{P+D}|^2}{|M_P|^2|M_D|^2},
1291	\end{equation}
1292	where $\vert M_P\vert^2$, $\vert M_D\vert^2$ and$|M_{P+D}|^2$ are, respectively, 
1293	the matrix-element squared for the production event, the decay event, and the decayed production event.
1294	In order to keep unweighted events after such re-weighting, we follow the MadSpin strategy of keeping 
1295	the same production event and try associating it with different decay events as long as none of them pass the unweighting criteria.
1296	
1297	This feature will be include in \mgamc~2.5.3  and is currently available on request. 
1298	The syntax for enacting such decays is provided in App.~\ref{app:MadSpin}.
1299

